3.0.4
Twenty-five items from the v3.0.2 verification round, five consumer sessions reporting, and one through-line: every gate here was keyed on a FORM, and each finding is another way a form fails to determine an effect. The argv prefix is now complete over its channel — every global before the subcommand is classified by whether it changes what the command RESOLVES to, six inert ones are allowed by name, and an UNKNOWN global is refused, because a future flag must not silently join the allow side. The preceding-clause blocklist became a three-way classification whose gated arms simply do not run on an inert clause, with the substitution, redirection and pipe exclusions holding the line — no clause is inert in a command containing a pipe, because `echo 'git merge …' | bash` executes. Finding 62 is the one nobody predicted and it changed the release's shape: `git -P merge feature/y` on a protected branch was ALLOWED on installed v3.0.2, on both gates, on four hosts, because `GC_GIT_PRE` in the lib tolerated only `-C` and `-c` and any other global made the clause not match as a merge at all — a fail-OPEN blind spot rather than a refusal. The third caller was worse in a way no exit code can show: `git -P commit -m x` matched no commit segment, so the commit gate RAN NO TESTS and exited 0 in zero seconds, indistinguishable from a green run — on a green suite both cases exit 0 and the `passed. (` marker is absent in the broken state AND in the fixed one, so the only channel that discriminates is the artifact's `path` field, `no-commit-segment` before the fix and `test` after. ONE fix, in the lib, shared by all three gates, which makes the ~90-minute three-parser matrix mandatory before the tag. Two corrections kept from building it: `-P` is NOT refused, because it is inert and refusing a flag IDEs pass unprompted is the shape that gets a guard switched off; and repeated `-C` is cumulative and RELATIVE — `git -C a -C b` resolves b from inside a — so "refuse more than one `-C`" would have failed the amendment's own want-0 row. `**Test**: none` BLOCKED EVERY COMMIT, measured: the value that reads as "I have no Test command" was the one value that hard-blocked, and the plan advised writing it; it is now the opt-out it looks like. And the actual source of a cycle of "auto mode is prompting" reports was neither the environment nor the pause counter but SIX DENY RULES: `Read(.env…)` entries are evaluated before the classifier, and a read-only command with a relative path after a `cd` cannot be statically proven not to hit one, so the harness prompted on ordinary reads and auto mode could not approve — replaced by a hook, which answers per call, with its blind spot (it sees arguments, not what an interpreter opens) stated in the DENY text and pinned by fixtures. Deferred and named rather than rushed: the Test path still has no terminal arm, because three shipped guards forbid clamping a 78 that has no provenance, and v3.0.4 carries the provenance channel they imply.
